WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.201 --> 00:00:02.368
-Our next guest
is a very funny actress,

00:00:02.368 --> 00:00:03.736
comedian, and writer
you know from shows like

00:00:03.736 --> 00:00:05.971
"Parks and Recreation"
and films like "Obvious Child."

00:00:05.971 --> 00:00:07.540
She stars in "The Great North."

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:10.743
The second season premieres
this Sunday night on -- on Fox.

00:00:10.743 --> 00:00:13.113
Excuse me.
Let's take a look.

00:00:13.113 --> 00:00:15.548
-[ Sighs ]
-Uh-oh. A flop and a sigh.

00:00:15.548 --> 00:00:18.017
What's going on, Jude?
-Ever since Dr. Gary

00:00:18.017 --> 00:00:21.087
said I'm getting my braces off,
I've felt...sad?

00:00:21.087 --> 00:00:23.155
-Well, you were always gonna
get them off eventually.

00:00:23.155 --> 00:00:24.924
It's a part of growing up.
-Like learning

00:00:24.924 --> 00:00:26.993
to shave your legs and then
not caring enough to do it.

00:00:26.993 --> 00:00:29.996
-But, Judy, once your teeth are
out of those little wire jails,

00:00:29.996 --> 00:00:31.764
you can do anything.

00:00:31.764 --> 00:00:35.067
Day after I got mine off, I went
right over to the car dealership

00:00:35.067 --> 00:00:36.936
and, through a series
of miscommunications,

00:00:36.936 --> 00:00:39.072
got trapped in a car trunk
for three days.

00:00:39.072 --> 00:00:40.340
-I guess I better
go look in the mirror

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:41.908
and sigh a little bit more.

00:00:41.908 --> 00:00:43.209
-Please welcome back
to the show,

00:00:43.209 --> 00:00:44.843
one of our favorites,
Jenny Slate!

00:00:44.843 --> 00:00:48.181
How are you, Jenny?
-Hi, Seth!

00:00:48.181 --> 00:00:50.884
Ooh!
[ Laughter ]

00:00:50.884 --> 00:00:53.286
Sorry. Nope.
I don't know, whatever.

00:00:53.286 --> 00:00:55.788
-I like that.
I think that energy was right.

00:00:55.788 --> 00:00:57.022
And not at all weird.
-Right?

00:00:57.022 --> 00:01:00.760
Well, I mean, I would love --
Okay. Hi, Seth.

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:03.096
Hi. That's really
a nice way to say "hi."

00:01:03.096 --> 00:01:06.265
Hi. I have a new baby,
and it seems like

00:01:06.265 --> 00:01:07.867
now when I say "hi" to adults,

00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:11.004
I say "hi" the way
that I do to my baby.

00:01:11.004 --> 00:01:14.473
And, like, so, a normal "hi"
for me at this point is like,

00:01:14.473 --> 00:01:17.844
[ Gasps ]
"Ooh! Hi!

00:01:17.844 --> 00:01:20.913
Who just woke up?"

00:01:20.913 --> 00:01:22.214
-This is very exciting.

00:01:22.214 --> 00:01:25.217
You had baby Ida in December,
correct?

00:01:25.217 --> 00:01:27.854
-I did.
I had her kind of like

00:01:27.854 --> 00:01:30.589
just a few days, I feel like,
after I last spoke with you.

00:01:30.589 --> 00:01:33.926
-Yes, you were very -- you
showed your belly on our show,

00:01:33.926 --> 00:01:35.795
which we really appreciated
seeing, because it did look

00:01:35.795 --> 00:01:38.197
like an any-day-now situation.

00:01:38.197 --> 00:01:40.699
-Yeah, the baby came out.
-Yeah.

00:01:40.699 --> 00:01:44.037
And how has it been?
-The stork did not come.

00:01:44.037 --> 00:01:47.606
It's really nice.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:47.606 --> 00:01:50.976
It's super nice to have a baby.

00:01:50.976 --> 00:01:57.784
I am, like, more tired than
I have ever, ever, ever been

00:01:57.784 --> 00:02:02.421
or known that a person
could be, like --

00:02:02.421 --> 00:02:04.657
Like, you know when
you're so tired that

00:02:04.657 --> 00:02:07.093
other things like you, like,
just put your head on a table

00:02:07.093 --> 00:02:08.594
and you're like,
"This could be a bed"?

00:02:08.594 --> 00:02:11.130
Like everything seems
like it could be a bed.

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:14.567
-Yep.
Are you finding time --

00:02:14.567 --> 00:02:17.804
I know one of the hardest
things, you know, for Lexi,

00:02:17.804 --> 00:02:20.473
when she gave birth was, like,
just finding time to, like,

00:02:20.473 --> 00:02:22.741
eat and do stuff like that.

00:02:22.741 --> 00:02:26.946
-Let me tell you, the other day,
I was on the telephone.

00:02:26.946 --> 00:02:28.781
I called up to speak
to someone at your show,

00:02:28.781 --> 00:02:30.249
and they were like,
"'Late Night.'

00:02:30.249 --> 00:02:32.652
Hold for one moment."
And I was like, "Oh, cool!"

00:02:32.652 --> 00:02:36.655
20 seconds maybe, I shoved half
a chicken thigh in my mouth.

00:02:36.655 --> 00:02:40.159
That's how I eat now.
It's like half of an animal,

00:02:40.159 --> 00:02:42.862
and I'm standing up,
and the whole time I go,

00:02:42.862 --> 00:02:46.299
"Sorry, sorry.
Sorry. Sorry."

00:02:46.299 --> 00:02:49.702
So, yeah, I find the time.
I do. I find it.

00:02:49.702 --> 00:02:52.805
-It must be both great
to be able to do voiceover work

00:02:52.805 --> 00:02:54.306
right now on a show like this,

00:02:54.306 --> 00:02:57.042
because you don't have to worry
about all the COVID protocols,

00:02:57.042 --> 00:03:00.013
but the negative must be
that you never get to interact

00:03:00.013 --> 00:03:02.748
with a cast that is so talented.

00:03:02.748 --> 00:03:08.854
-It's such a bummer because,
also, we started in the studio.

00:03:08.854 --> 00:03:10.823
So, like, you know how not --

00:03:10.823 --> 00:03:12.558
we know how much fun
it could have been.

00:03:12.558 --> 00:03:16.229
I like to compare it to my
shellfish and avocado allergy,

00:03:16.229 --> 00:03:19.265
which is like, there was a time
when I ingested it a lot,

00:03:19.265 --> 00:03:21.366
and I was like, "Oh, these
things are very delicious,"

00:03:21.366 --> 00:03:24.136
and then suddenly I got, like,
crazy explosive diarrhea,

00:03:24.136 --> 00:03:26.605
and I could
never have them again.

00:03:26.605 --> 00:03:30.843
Not that I'm allergic to our
cast, but now we're over Zoom --

00:03:30.843 --> 00:03:33.612
But I will say it's really nice
because you have that energy

00:03:33.612 --> 00:03:36.081
of, like, people that
are super eager to perform.

00:03:36.081 --> 00:03:39.552
-Yes.
-That like jacked-up energy

00:03:39.552 --> 00:03:42.755
and then also all of the squares
of the Fox executives

00:03:42.755 --> 00:03:46.225
that just have their names
and they're just, like, lurking.

00:03:46.225 --> 00:03:48.394
-Do you like the lurkers?

00:03:48.394 --> 00:03:50.296
-Yeah, I mean,
I hate to admit it.

00:03:50.296 --> 00:03:52.365
I never thought that
that could be something

00:03:52.365 --> 00:03:54.533
I would get into,
but I'm just like,

00:03:54.533 --> 00:03:56.769
"I wonder what Todd
is thinking about this."

00:03:56.769 --> 00:03:59.305
[ Laughter ]

00:03:59.305 --> 00:04:01.307
"What does Todd look like?
Like, is he chuckling?

00:04:01.307 --> 00:04:02.709
He's so withholding.
I'll never know.

00:04:02.709 --> 00:04:04.677
I better floor it
with my performance."

00:04:04.677 --> 00:04:06.012
[ Laughter ]

00:04:06.012 --> 00:04:07.814
-You -- Your character,
we found out,

00:04:07.814 --> 00:04:09.849
was obsessed
with Alanis Morissette,

00:04:09.849 --> 00:04:12.919
and you got to meet via Zoom.

00:04:12.919 --> 00:04:14.887
Was it via Zoom
that you met Alanis?

00:04:14.887 --> 00:04:18.390
-No, it was in person.
-You met her in person.

00:04:18.390 --> 00:04:20.226
That is outstanding.

00:04:20.226 --> 00:04:21.560
Now, I know
she was a big deal to you.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:24.830
Did you keep your cool?
-I mean, what do you think?

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:26.332
You know,
you know me a little bit.

00:04:26.332 --> 00:04:27.600
[ Both laugh ]

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:30.402
-I think you are smart enough
and aware enough

00:04:30.402 --> 00:04:32.204
to at least go in
with the preparation

00:04:32.204 --> 00:04:35.641
of trying not to lose your cool.
-For sure, of course.

00:04:35.641 --> 00:04:39.812
I was like, "Today, Alanis will
be at the table read.

00:04:39.812 --> 00:04:41.214
Let's -- You know,
I don't need to, like,

00:04:41.214 --> 00:04:44.150
wear my
"Jagged Little Pill" shirt.

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:47.619
I can wear a blue turtleneck,
so that she'll think, like,

00:04:47.619 --> 00:04:50.189
that I'm all --
I'm, like, just normal.

00:04:50.189 --> 00:04:52.424
I'm just waking up.
I didn't, like, shoot out of bed

00:04:52.424 --> 00:04:53.559
and hit the ceiling

00:04:53.559 --> 00:04:55.461
because Alanis
will be in the same room."

00:04:55.461 --> 00:04:59.065
And I will also say, I don't
really like to meet, like,

00:04:59.065 --> 00:05:01.867
people that I admire.
-Mm-hmm.

00:05:01.867 --> 00:05:04.570
-Because --
Because if it goes wrong,

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:06.739
then the whole thing
is, like, ruined forever.

00:05:06.739 --> 00:05:11.343
Like, I one time
met this jazz singer

00:05:11.343 --> 00:05:14.980
who I really liked,
who was very old.

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:18.317
And now she is deceased,
not that that's part of this,

00:05:18.317 --> 00:05:20.352
but it's a part of life.
-Yes.

00:05:20.352 --> 00:05:21.454
[ Both laugh ]

00:05:21.454 --> 00:05:23.756
-So, you know,
we all go eventually.

00:05:23.756 --> 00:05:29.028
But I, like, had my little CD,
because this was in the past,

00:05:29.028 --> 00:05:31.664
and I went up to her,
and I was like,

00:05:31.664 --> 00:05:35.968
"I just want you to know, your
music means so much to me."

00:05:35.968 --> 00:05:38.404
And this, like, tiny little
old lady looked over

00:05:38.404 --> 00:05:41.774
to the other like 100-year-old
person next to her and went,

00:05:41.774 --> 00:05:46.746
"Oh, brother!"
[ Laughter ]

00:05:46.746 --> 00:05:50.582
I was like...
[ Gasping ]

00:05:50.582 --> 00:05:53.219
And so, I was afraid to meet
Alanis in case she hated me

00:05:53.219 --> 00:05:56.388
like that old woman did hate me
before she died.

00:05:56.388 --> 00:05:57.790
-Yeah.
-And, um --

00:05:57.790 --> 00:06:00.559
And so I was like,
"Just don't do anything.

00:06:00.559 --> 00:06:03.362
Just sit next to Alanis
and just don't do anything.

00:06:03.362 --> 00:06:06.399
Just say your lines."
And so I went in there,

00:06:06.399 --> 00:06:08.567
I sat next to Alanis,
and I was like --

00:06:08.567 --> 00:06:10.536
[ Laughing ]
"I love you so much!

00:06:10.536 --> 00:06:12.638
Would you want to take
a picture with m-e-e?!"

00:06:12.638 --> 00:06:15.040
And, um -- And she did.

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:16.509
And she did,
and we took a selfie.

00:06:16.509 --> 00:06:19.512
-Okay, good.
So that one had a happy ending.

00:06:19.512 --> 00:06:23.382
-Yeah, yeah.
I mean, the picture is uncool.

00:06:23.382 --> 00:06:26.785
-Gotcha. But at least --
It's like proof of life.

00:06:26.785 --> 00:06:30.489
You did meet her,
and you can prove it to people.

00:06:30.489 --> 00:06:32.191
-Yeah. For sure.
I can prove it

00:06:32.191 --> 00:06:35.127
if I want to show a picture
of Alanis looking normal

00:06:35.127 --> 00:06:37.796
and me going...
[ Laughter ]

00:06:37.796 --> 00:06:41.801
Like, flared nostrils,
like weird, as if I have an itch

00:06:41.801 --> 00:06:43.869
and I'm trying to make a face
to make it go away

00:06:43.869 --> 00:06:44.971
rather than scratch it.

00:06:44.971 --> 00:06:47.106
Like, it's just
not a good picture.

00:06:47.106 --> 00:06:48.975
But I have the picture,

00:06:48.975 --> 00:06:51.877
and Alanis is really nice,
of course.

00:06:51.877 --> 00:06:53.946
-You -- I want
to close by asking this.

00:06:53.946 --> 00:06:56.349
I know that you have been
trying to get married,

00:06:56.349 --> 00:06:59.018
and COVID has been throwing
wrench after wrench

00:06:59.018 --> 00:07:00.653
in your plans.
But you were kind of --

00:07:00.653 --> 00:07:02.922
You might be pulling it off,
finally.

00:07:02.922 --> 00:07:06.158
-Yeah, that's true.
We've cancelled three times.

00:07:06.158 --> 00:07:10.596
Not because of arguments
but because of a pandemic,

00:07:10.596 --> 00:07:13.232
which is a lovely way
to cancel your wedding.

00:07:13.232 --> 00:07:17.403
-Yeah.
-And I think we're gonna do it

00:07:17.403 --> 00:07:19.739
just in a much smaller way,
like, in our living room.

00:07:19.739 --> 00:07:22.074
That's the way now.
-We've talked in the past

00:07:22.074 --> 00:07:24.309
about how one of your
favorite things about weddings

00:07:24.309 --> 00:07:28.080
is when the bride surprises
the groom with a song

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:30.816
that she has written about him,
and how the groom

00:07:30.816 --> 00:07:33.553
is, in the moment,
devastated it's happening.

00:07:33.553 --> 00:07:37.123
Have you thought about
doing this for Ben?

00:07:37.123 --> 00:07:38.858
-Oh, you know, yeah.
I mean, when your wedding

00:07:38.858 --> 00:07:40.659
gets ruined three times,
you're like,

00:07:40.659 --> 00:07:45.031
"Let's just kill this thing."
You know, like, I --

00:07:45.031 --> 00:07:47.867
And just to --
What I -- Just to say,

00:07:47.867 --> 00:07:52.405
I don't dislike it when someone,
like, sings a song with --

00:07:52.405 --> 00:07:55.708
Like, I was at a wedding.
A bride sang a song

00:07:55.708 --> 00:07:58.077
with the groom on the piano.

00:07:58.077 --> 00:08:00.546
I was like, "This is
a wonderful version of this."

00:08:00.546 --> 00:08:01.914
What I don't like is, like,

00:08:01.914 --> 00:08:03.482
bride at the top
of a staircase --

00:08:03.482 --> 00:08:06.052
Why is there
a staircase in your venue?

00:08:06.052 --> 00:08:11.524
Mic -- wireless mic
and singing a specific song

00:08:11.524 --> 00:08:12.958
just like...

00:08:12.958 --> 00:08:16.895
♪ Troy, I let you go
on the fishing trip with Kevin ♪

00:08:16.895 --> 00:08:18.965
♪ Because you know
that I trust you ♪

00:08:18.965 --> 00:08:20.599
♪ Trust is essential ♪

00:08:20.599 --> 00:08:23.702
Or whatever, and it's just like,
"This isn't for him."

00:08:23.702 --> 00:08:25.837
That bothered me.
-If you --

00:08:25.837 --> 00:08:28.307
Would you like to rehearse
or at least show us

00:08:28.307 --> 00:08:30.676
a little taste of what --
if you did this --

00:08:30.676 --> 00:08:32.144
what you would sing to Ben?

00:08:32.144 --> 00:08:35.247
-Yeah, because here's the thing.
I already had the baby.

00:08:35.247 --> 00:08:36.381
-Yeah.
-You know?

00:08:36.381 --> 00:08:39.184
So, it's like he's here.

00:08:39.184 --> 00:08:41.620
So I feel like,
instead of it being like...

00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:43.522
♪ When I met you,
it was summer ♪

00:08:43.522 --> 00:08:46.258
♪ A beautiful season ♪
...or whatever.

00:08:46.258 --> 00:08:49.462
If you like summer.
It would more be like...

00:08:49.462 --> 00:08:53.799
♪ Ben, you saw me poop
in the barf bag ♪

00:08:53.799 --> 00:08:56.602
♪ Because I couldn't
get to the bathroom ♪

00:08:56.602 --> 00:09:01.507
♪ When the baby exploded
from my vag-i-i-ina ♪

00:09:01.507 --> 00:09:04.209
♪ I love you, baby ♪
Right?

00:09:04.209 --> 00:09:07.145
-I think everyone
in that living room

00:09:07.145 --> 00:09:10.115
will jump to their feet
if you sing that song.

00:09:10.115 --> 00:09:11.884
-Yeah, I mean, I'm gonna be --
even though it's gonna be

00:09:11.884 --> 00:09:13.552
in our living room,
and I'm not gonna need

00:09:13.552 --> 00:09:15.954
that amplification,
I'm gonna be on the mic.

00:09:15.954 --> 00:09:17.022
-You're gonna be
on a wireless mic.

00:09:17.022 --> 00:09:18.390
I think
that's an excellent call.

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:20.392
Hey, Jenny, it's always
so lovely to see you.

00:09:20.392 --> 00:09:21.927
Thanks so much for being here.

00:09:21.927 --> 00:09:24.496
-Thanks for having me, Seth.
-The second season of

00:09:24.496 --> 00:09:27.700
"The Great North" premieres this
Sunday at 8:30 p.m. on Fox.

